editorialMd:
Build the result list

A prefix is the start of a word. For example, "c", "ca", and "cat" are the prefixes with at least one letter of "cat". For each word in words, look at all its prefixes with at least one letter. Count how many input words start with each prefix, then add those counts. Count repeated input words separately, and include the word itself. Put one total per word in the result list, in input order.

Return the answer

Count the even numbers in the result list. An even number divides by 2 with no remainder. Count 0 and negative even numbers too. Return the count. An empty list gives 0.

Why this works

Each value in the result list follows Step 1. Step 2 uses only those values. Keeping these steps separate helps avoid returning the list when the question asks for one number. Check empty input, one item, repeated values, and the largest allowed input.

Efficiency goal: time O(total characters); extra memory, including the result list, O(n). n is the input length; total characters means all word lengths added together. These labels describe how work and memory grow with the input.

javascript
function prefixCabinetEvenKeyCensus(words, k) {
  const a=words; let out=[];
  const next=[new Map()],counts=[0];for(const word of a){let node=0;for(const c of word){if(!next[node].has(c)){next[node].set(c,next.length);next.push(new Map());counts.push(0);}node=next[node].get(c);counts[node]++;}}for(const word of a){let node=0,s=0;for(const c of word){node=next[node].get(c);s+=counts[node];}out.push(s);}
  return out.filter(x=>x%2===0).length;
}


